e on this and white's going to  have a very good game so first let's  look at what white's going to do well  they only have one option that's to play  king 2 d1  and now where does  black continue to because we have so  many threats we have the threat of  knight to c7 check forking the rook on  a8  we have the threats of queen to g7  preparing to take this rook on h8 and  yes this bishop and the queen did attack  on f2 but after that they can't really  do anything else the queen can't come to  f1 it's being protected by the light  squared bishop it can't come to e1  because  black doesn't have control of the e1  square except the queen  can't take this knight here can't take  the pawn on g2 right away because the  queen is protected so how do they  continue well let's look at a few  options one let's say they start with g6  but if they move g6 we can now introduce  an additional threat we can play knight  h3 attacking their queen  and where do they go from here well if  they tried something like d6 and say hey  you can take my queen but then i can  just take your queen the problem why  that's not going to work for black is we  can now take with our knight and then  when they take our queen with their  bishop we can now take with our knights  now they can castle on the queen side  but you can see come over we have four  minor pieces they have three minor  pieces they did lose that exchange but  there's just no good square for them to  go to so let's come back what if they  don't play  g6 what if instead they play king to d8  remember they have to worry about so  many threats one of those threats is  knight to c7 forking the king and  attacking the rook on a8  but now we can just play queen takes on  g7  they could come down to  d4 this is definitely an option  but now we can go ahead and take on  h8  because our queen is no longer  protecting the g2 square they can swing  over and take on g2 but now we can play  the move knight to e3 which is such a  brilliant move a